  "sections": {
    "shape": [
      {
        "id": "forty-characters",
        "text": "The secret access key paired with an access key ID is 40 characters of the base64 alphabet (letters, digits, plus and slash), with no prefix, lexical marker or checksum. A temporary (ASIA) credential carries a secret of the same shape. AWS's documentation example has this shape, and the IAM AccessKey API types the field only as a string.",
        "evidenceClass": "provider-documented",
        "temporality": "current",
        "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
        "citations": [
          {
            "kind": "claim",
            "claimId": "field-value"
          },
          {
            "kind": "source",
            "sourceId": "docs-aws-amazon-com-1e9489a68f"
          },
          {
            "kind": "source",
            "sourceId": "docs-aws-amazon-com-93c1588af6"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "thirty-bytes",
        "text": "A 2018 write-up describes the 40 characters as base64 of 30 random bytes, which is why no padding character appears inside the 40.",
        "evidenceClass": "tool-corroborated",
        "temporality": "current",
        "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
        "citations": [
          {
            "kind": "claim",
            "claimId": "field-padding"
          },
          {
            "kind": "source",
            "sourceId": "summitroute-com-f8a30bb19a"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "padding-disagreement",
        "text": "One AWS-owned scanner rule allows an equals sign in the 40 characters and another omits it; standard base64 has no equals sign inside 40 characters for 30 bytes, so the equals sign is not part of the value.",
        "evidenceClass": "tool-corroborated",
        "temporality": "current",
        "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
        "citations": [
          {
            "kind": "claim",
            "claimId": "field-padding"
          },
          {
            "kind": "source",
            "sourceId": "github-com-awslabs-git-secrets-57d690e6e4"
          },
          {
            "kind": "source",
            "sourceId": "github-com-yelp-detect-secrets-385a46e487"
          },
          {
            "kind": "source",
            "sourceId": "github-com-trufflesecurity-trufflehog-8cfe179606"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "issuance": [
      {
        "id": "not-issued",
        "text": "No secret access key was issued or observed for this record, so the shape rests on documentation examples, AWS-owned code and independent implementations.",
        "evidenceClass": "unresolved",
        "temporality": "current",
        "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
        "unresolved": {
          "reviewEvent": 2,
          "reason": "No key was created or observed for this research."
        }
      }
    ],
    "collisions": [
      {
        "id": "bare-value-ambiguous",
        "text": "Any 40-character base64 run is also a hash, a path or an ordinary token, so a bare value does not identify an AWS secret. The supportable reading is a value assigned to an AWS-named key or sitting beside an AKIA or ASIA key ID; the reviewed rules disagree on the key-name list and on quoting, and none treats the bare value as a finding without its neighbour.",
        "evidenceClass": "tool-corroborated",
        "temporality": "current",
        "observedAt": "2026-09-29",
        "citations": [
          {
            "kind": "claim",
            "claimId": "field-context"
          },
          {
